From 5b9ffdc0d959af136f6b35e1d51db89abaae2cea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 07:53:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DVDInputStreamBluray: a seamless join is known by where the
 read that tells it began, also when it read nothing

libbluray-9090 makes the read that reaches the end of a play item return nothing, with the events of
the next play item, and the bytes of its clip from the next read on, so that a demuxer that the player
opens for the new play item gets them all. Until now that read returned the first bytes of the new clip
with BD_EVENT_PLAYITEM, and with the disc's menus a play item whose streams are opened again lost them
to the demuxer of the play item before: the new demuxer started inside the PES packet of the clip's first
random-access picture, and its video began only at the next one.

BlurayJoin.h took those bytes as the sign that the play item was reached by reading and not jumped to,
and with libbluray-9090 it would have opened the streams again at every seamless join. A join is now
known by what can be checked either way: libbluray tells the play item with a read of the disc (not with
the events of a key press that the thread of the application left for this one: NOT_READ, as before),
and that read began where the play item begins in the playlist, whether it returned the first bytes of
the clip (a libbluray without libbluray-9090) or nothing (with it: where libbluray is). A jump lands
elsewhere; one that lands on the start of the next play item is a seek, and libbluray queues
BD_EVENT_SEEK after the play item (_seek_internal()): the player holds on that one and opens the streams
again, with nothing read in between.

Run on the host (the join test, nav_join_bd.cpp, with
Kodi's 6144-byte reads and two new checks for each play item whose streams are opened again): with
libbluray-9090 and this, the seamless join of playlist 1 goes on as before, and the demuxers opened for
the second play items of playlists 2 and 3 start with the first video packet of their clips, while the
demuxers before them get nothing of those clips (31 of 31). Without this, libbluray-9090 opens the
streams again at the seamless join (8 checks fail); without libbluray-9090, the new demuxers start 0.125 s
into their clips and the old ones get a packet of the new clips (4 fail), which is what the image does
now. Decide() on its own: 9 checks, and two mutations of it fail.
